course: books
lesson: 31
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter II, part 3: The Open Road (life on the road, and a motor-car)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: First they must catch the old grey horse, who was never asked and would much rather stay in his field. Then they set off on a golden afternoon, birds singing, travellers saying nice things about the cart, and rabbits gasping «O my! O my! O my!» from the hedges.
explain: That night, on a lonely common under the stars, Toad boasts: «This is the real life for a gentleman! Talk about your old river!» The Rat says quietly that he doesn't talk about his river — but he thinks about it all the time. The Mole squeezes his paw in the dark and offers to run home with him at dawn. No, says the Rat — he must stick by Toad till it's over; it won't take long.
explain: Next morning Toad sleeps and sleeps, while the Rat and the Mole do all the work, the Mole walking miles for milk and eggs Toad forgot. Toad strolls out fresh and says how easy life is now! The next day they make him work — and suddenly he's much less keen, and has to be dragged out of his bunk.
explain: On the third afternoon they come out on their first high-road. A faint hum like a bee, a little cloud of dust, a sad «Poop-poop!» — and suddenly a huge, glittering motor-car roars past, blasting them with wind and dust, and shrinks back to a speck in the distance.
fact: The old grey horse had, «without having been consulted, and to his own extreme annoyance», been «told off by Toad for the dustiest job» — and «took a deal of catching» (consulted = asked; told off = given the job).
fact: «It was a golden afternoon. The smell of the dust they kicked up was rich and satisfying»; rabbits in the hedgerows «held up their fore-paws, and said, "O my! O my! O my!"»
fact: They camped «on a remote common far from habitations» (a common = open land anyone may use; habitations = houses).
fact: Toad: «Well, good night, you fellows! This is the real life for a gentleman! Talk about your old river!»
fact: The Rat: «I don't talk about my river ... You know I don't, Toad. But I think about it ... I think about it—all the time!» — he is homesick.
fact: The Mole: «Shall we run away to-morrow morning, quite early—very early—and go back to our dear old hole on the river?»
fact: The Rat: «No, no, we'll see it out ... I ought to stick by Toad till this trip is ended. It wouldn't be safe for him to be left to himself. It won't take very long. His fads never do.»
fact: «The end was indeed nearer than even the Rat suspected.» — the writer warns us something is coming.
fact: Next morning Toad «slept very soundly, and no amount of shaking could rouse him»; the Mole «trudged off to the nearest village, a long way off, for milk and eggs and various necessaries the Toad had, of course, forgotten».
fact: Toad appeared «fresh and gay, remarking what a pleasant easy life it was», after «the cares and worries and fatigues of housekeeping at home» (gay here = cheerful, its old meaning).
fact: When made to do his share, Toad «was by no means so rapturous about the simplicity of the primitive life» and «attempted to resume his place in his bunk, whence he was hauled by force».
fact: The horse «had complained that he was being frightfully left out of it», so the Mole walked at his head talking to him.
fact: The car came first as «a faint warning hum; like the drone of a distant bee», then «a small cloud of dust, with a dark centre of energy», and «a faint "Poop-poop!" wailed like an uneasy animal in pain».
fact: Then «the magnificent motor-car, immense, breath-snatching, passionate, with its pilot tense and hugging his wheel» flashed by with «glittering plate-glass and rich morocco» (morocco = fine leather) and «dwindled to a speck in the far distance, changed back into a droning bee once more».
fact: Old words: «trudging» = walking heavily; «necessaries» = things you need; «turned in» = went to bed; «whence» = from where; «fleet and unforeseen» = fast and unexpected; «after-career» = the rest of his life.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «yeah nah» = no; «sweet as» = great; «toque» (Canada) = woolly hat; «mid» = only so-so; «howay» (North East England) = come on; «servo» (Australia) = petrol station.
exercise: Who was never asked whether he wanted to come? | The old grey horse | The Mole, his new friend | The bird in the cage | The Water Rat himself
exercise: Why does the horse take «a deal of catching»? | He'd rather stay in his field | He is too old to walk anywhere | He's asleep | He's afraid of Toad
exercise: What kind of afternoon is it when they set off? | A golden afternoon | A grey, drizzly afternoon with thunder in the hills | A snowy afternoon | A windy evening
exercise: What do the rabbits in the hedgerows say? | «O my! O my! O my!» | «Onion-sauce!» | «Sixpence, please» | «Poop-poop!»
exercise: Where do they camp the first night? | On a remote common | In a town inn with a soft bed for each of them | By the river | In the Wild Wood
exercise: What does Toad say at bedtime? | «This is the real life for a gentleman!» | «I miss my house» | «I'm cold» | «Let's go home first thing tomorrow»
exercise: What does the Rat say about his river? | He doesn't talk about it, but thinks about it all the time | He has forgotten it completely and loves the road much better | He hates it | Nothing
exercise: What does the Mole do in the dark? | Squeezes the Rat's paw | Sings the Ducks' Ditty quietly | Snores | Goes outside
exercise: What does the Mole offer? | To run back to the river early next morning | To drive the cart himself all the way to the sea | To cook breakfast | To wake Toad
exercise: Why won't the Rat leave? | It wouldn't be safe to leave Toad alone | He has started to love the open road more than his river | He's lost | He promised Badger
exercise: «The end was indeed nearer than even the Rat suspected.» What does this sentence do? | Warns us something is about to happen | Says the road came to an end at a wall | Ends the book | Ends the day
exercise: What does Toad do on the first morning? | Sleeps and can't be woken | Gets up first and cooks a big breakfast for everyone | Leaves | Writes a song
exercise: Where does the Mole walk to on the first morning? | The nearest village | Back down to the river | All the way to Toad Hall | The town with the railway station
exercise: What had Toad forgotten to pack? | Milk, eggs and other necessaries | Biscuits, sardines and potted lobster | The bird | The map
exercise: What does Toad say when he finally appears? | What a pleasant, easy life it is | How tired he is from all the work | Sorry | Let's go home
exercise: What happens on the second evening? | They make Toad do his fair share | Toad does all the work happily and sings | They go home | They meet Badger
exercise: How does Toad leave his bunk the next morning? | He is hauled out by force | He jumps out early, eager to go | He is woken by the bird | He doesn't
exercise: Why is the Mole walking at the horse's head on the high-road? | The horse complained of being left out | The horse is lost | The Mole is driving | Toad told him to
exercise: What does the motor-car sound like at first, far away? | The drone of a distant bee | A thunderstorm rolling over the downs | A train | Ducks quacking
exercise: What sound comes out of the dust? | «Poop-poop!» | «Sploosh!» | «cloop!» | «Hooray!»
exercise: «glittering plate-glass and rich morocco». What is morocco? | Fine leather | A kind of shiny metal used on wheels | A country's flag | A coloured glass
exercise: What happens to the car in the end? | It dwindles to a speck | It stops and the driver says sorry to them all | It crashes into a big tree | It turns round and comes back
test: Joe, howay (come on) man: «Toad did all the work on the trip, he's a grafter.» (grafter = hard worker) Put it right. | The Rat and the Mole did it | Right: he got up first every morning and did every job while the others slept | The horse did it all | Nobody worked
test: «This is the real life for a gentleman!» What's funny about Toad saying it? | He's had the easy part while others did the work | He's really a hard-working gentleman who earned his rest | He's a horse | He's lying about his name
test: The Rat says «I think about it—all the time!» What is he feeling? | Homesick for his river | Excited about the car he's going to buy | Sleepy | Angry at the Mole
test: Why does the Rat stay with Toad though he's homesick? | He's a loyal friend who thinks Toad needs looking after | He secretly loves the open road and doesn't want the trip to end | Toad pays him | He's lost
test: Lucy, mid (so-so) about the trip: «the horse loved it, he volunteered.» Put it right. | He wasn't asked and was annoyed | Right: he trotted up and asked to come, as he hated his paddock | The horse stayed home | There was no horse
test: Why is Toad «by no means so rapturous» after the second day? | He had to do his share of the work | He was ill | The cart broke | It rained all day and night
test: The writer says the car is «immense, breath-snatching, passionate». Does the writer describe it like a machine or like a wild animal? | Like a wild animal | Like a quiet, dull machine that nobody would notice | Like a singing bird | Like a slow river boat
test: The car flashed past very close to the cart. Why was that dangerous? | It could have hit them | It wasn't: the car stopped politely and waited | They'd have caught a lift | They'd have flown into the air
test: Looking back: what did the Otter say Toad had «no» of in chapter I? | Stability | Money in the bank | Good friends | Boats of his own, because he sold them all to buy the caravan
test: Looking back: what did the Rat say when Toad asked him to come? | «I'm not coming, and that's flat» | «Yes, at once!» | «Ask the Mole» | «Only if we take the boat»
test: Looking back: in chapter I, who else said «O my! O my! O my!» besides these rabbits? | The Mole, at the backwater and the picnic | The Otter, at the mustard pot | Badger | Toad
test: Looking back: in chapter I, the Mole tipped a boat over because of pride. In this part, who is being selfish? | Toad, who leaves all the work to others | The Mole again, who refuses to fetch the milk and eggs | The Rat | The horse
test: Looking back: what did the Rat promise Toad's crazes always do? | Never take very long | Last for ever | Make him rich | Make him wise
test: ★★★ The motor-car is a bee at the start and «a droning bee once more» at the end. Why does the writer do this? | It's far away at both ends, near only for a flash | It really is a bee | The car turns into a bee by magic | The bee is chasing it
test: ★★★ How many nights do they spend on the road before the motor-car (the car comes on the third afternoon)? | 2 | 3 | 1 | 4
test: ★★★ «disaster momentous indeed to their expedition, but simply overwhelming in its effect on the after-career of Toad». What does the writer hint? | This changes the rest of Toad's life | The cart will be fine | Toad will go home and forget it | Nobody will be hurt or changed
exam: Identify the creature which, without having been consulted, was assigned the dustiest task of the expedition. | The old grey horse | The Mole | The Rat | The bird in the bird-cage
exam: Ascertain the sentiment expressed by the Rat concerning his river on the first night. | That he did not speak of it but thought of it continually | That he had wholly forgotten it | That he despised it | That he intended to sell it
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises Toad's conduct on the first morning. | He slept soundly while his companions performed the labour | He rose early and prepared breakfast for all | He departed for the village | He groomed the horse
exam: Identify the first indication of the approach of the motor-car. | A faint warning hum like the drone of a distant bee | A crash | A shout from the driver | The bird singing
